                     KillPFile v1.0 (4-Jul-96) by Fate


iNTRODUCTiON
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
KillPFile is a hack to free CNet ports from hanging if a door forgot to run
through  the  Shutdown()  routine.   It  manually  asks  the port specified
through arguments to shut down(), which would have normally been the job of
the door.

KillPFile was born on suggestions by Shaun Gamble (Verbal Verbatim BBS,
Australia) and help from Ray Akey, the new CNet/4 owner and developer.


REQUiREMENTS
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Any  CNet  version  and  OS  2.0  or higher.  If anybody could test it with
CNet/4 please mail me your experiences.


fEATURES
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
o Small and fast 'cause it's NOT using the standard SAS/C startup code
o Optionally lists all currently active CNet Message Ports
o Sets Return Codes for Scripting
o Not even a single config file
o Another fine 1oo% product! :)


iNSTALLATiON
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Keep it whereever you like.  I recommend the "PFiles:1oo/KillPFile/" dir.


cONFiGURATiON
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
What configuration?


uSAGE
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
KillPFile is subject to be run from within a shell.

KillPFile                - lists all currently active MsgPorts for CNet C
                           door communication

KillPFile PORT=x DOOR=y  - shuts the specified MsgPort down. x is the port
                           and y the door instance (remember, you can run
                           several PFiles at the same time) the MsgPort was
                           created for.

So, if your favourite game just hang or you were debugging your new program
using CPR and my CDM debugging module and by accident hit 'q' use KillPFile
without  arguments  to  find  the correct port and door settings and simply
kill it :)

Notes: KillPFile was written in a few hours and is more a quick hack than a
       well-tested program.  It may, and it certainly once will, crash your
       system.  Imagine if you shutdown a port and the door you thought of
       to be hanging comes back to life and now wants to clean up its
       resources...

       When  listing  the  available  ports  KillPFile directly operates on
       Exec's  MsgPort List, only saved by Disable() and Enable() calls.  A
       MsgPort  listed  here  may  have  meanwhile  disappeared when you're
       trying to kill it.


HiSTORY
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
7-Jul-95    v1.0    First version


